From 3d71b97faa981143b9dce44242ab567399c2186d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Iaroslav Voitovych <yaroslav.voytovych@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 13:49:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Bluetooth: hci_conn: synchronize with SCO disconnect work
 before reuse

hci_conn_hold() cancels a pending disconnect with cancel_delayed_work(),
which does not wait for hci_conn_timeout() if it is already running.
hci_conn_timeout() reads refcnt without hdev->lock and, if it saw no
holder, calls hci_abort_conn(), which sets abort_reason.

A connect() that finds the SCO connection after the work saw no holder,
but before abort_reason is set, passes the abort_reason check in
hci_connect_sco(), takes its hold, and gets a connection that is
aborted right after: connect() reports success at once and the socket
is closed with ECONNABORTED when the Disconnection Complete arrives.
This applies both to a connection whose link exists and to one the
remote device set up, which gets its link from hci_conn_link() here.

After taking the hold, wait for a running hci_conn_timeout() and check
abort_reason again. For a SCO connection hci_conn_timeout() does not
take hdev->lock: hci_proto_disconn_ind() returns a constant and
hci_abort_conn() only queues the Disconnect. hci_conn_del() already
waits for disc_work with hdev->lock held. For a new connection the work
has never been queued and nothing is waited for. Once the wait returns,
the work has finished and cannot be queued again while the caller's
hold is taken, so the second check does not race with it. If the
connection had no link before, the link hci_conn_link() just made stays
with the aborting connection and is released with its ACL hold by
hci_conn_del(), as for any linked SCO connection.

A connect() that comes before the work has started at all still cancels
it with its hold and keeps the live link, as it does without this
patch; that case is not changed here.

Reproduced by delaying hci_conn_timeout() after it has seen no holder
(a debug-only change, not part of this patch), for a link set up by the
host and for one set up by the remote device: without this patch
connect() returns 0 and the socket is then closed with ECONNABORTED;
with it, connect() fails with -ECONNABORTED.

Fixes: e6720779ae61 ("Bluetooth: SCO: Use kref to track lifetime of sco_conn")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: Iaroslav Voitovych <yaroslav.voytovych@gmail.com>
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
index 6b92fd0177857..c1c8d2b29e3c0 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_conn.c
@@ -1935,6 +1935,17 @@ struct hci_conn *hci_connect_sco(struct hci_dev *hdev, int type, bdaddr_t *dst,
 		}
 	}
 
+	/* Taking the hold cannot stop a hci_conn_timeout() that is already
+	 * running: it may have seen no holder and be about to abort the
+	 * connection. It does not take hdev->lock, so wait for it and check
+	 * abort_reason again.
+	 */
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&sco->disc_work);
+	if (sco->abort_reason) {
+		hci_conn_drop(sco);
+		return ERR_PTR(-ECONNABORTED);
+	}
+
 	sco->setting = setting;
 	sco->codec = *codec;
 
